--------------------------------- KEY MECHANISM EXPLAINED ---------------------------------

Three Arrays Working Together:

1. arr[] - Stores actual values
   - Values are placed at dynamically allocated indices
   - Same array holds values for all k stacks

2. top[] - Tracks top of each stack
   - top[i] = index in arr[] where top of stack i resides
   - -1 means stack is empty

3. next[] - Dual purpose
   - For elements in a stack: points to next element below
   - For free slots: forms linked list of available indices

Free List Management:
- Initially: 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> ... -> n-1 -> null
- Push: Remove from front of free list
- Pop: Add back to front of free list
- Ensures O(1) allocation and deallocation

Stack as Linked List:
- Each stack is a linked list within the array
- Elements are not necessarily contiguous in arr[]
- Linked via next[] array
- Allows dynamic growth anywhere in array


--------------------------------- WHY THIS IS OPTIMAL ---------------------------------

vs Naive Fixed-Segment Approach:

Naive (Divide array into k equal parts):
- Each stack gets n/k slots
- Stack i overflows at n/k even if other stacks are empty
- Wasted space: up to (k-1) * (n/k) slots

Your Approach (Dynamic Linked List):
- All stacks share entire array
- Stack i can use all n slots if others are empty
- Zero waste until all n slots are used
- Overflow only when entire array is full

Example (n=100, k=3):
- Naive: Stack 0 overflows at 34, even if Stack 1 and 2 are empty (66 slots wasted)
- Your approach: Stack 0 can use all 100 slots if others empty (0 waste)


